# Why does Rust allow many immutable borrows but only one mutable?

Short answer: The combination Rust forbids is not reading plus writing, it’s aliasing plus mutation. Shared borrows can’t change anything, so no reader can invalidate another and any number of them can be alive at once. A mutable borrow can change everything, including where the value’s memory lives, so it has to be exclusive: while it exists, nothing else may be looking at the value.

## Readers can’t invalidate each other

A `&` hands out read access and nothing else. Two shared borrows see the same bytes from the first line to the last, and neither can be the reason the other goes wrong. That’s why the rule puts no number on them: any count of `&` borrows is fine. The compiler only asks whether a `&mut` overlaps one.

## A writer can move the memory under a reader’s feet

